# Migrations that are deliberately NOT applied to production, one id per line.
#
# The bundle stamps how many migrations it expects, and the drift banner fires
# when production has fewer. Counting a migration that is intentionally on the
# shelf makes that warning permanent — and a warning that is always on is one
# nobody reads on the day it is finally true.
#
# They stay in this directory on purpose: tests/database.mjs enumerates the
# folder, and that is the only thing exercising them before they go live.
#
# Remove the line in the same change that applies the migration.
202609090026_intimate_games

# 202609140034 puts two AFTER triggers on `messages` — the hottest write path in
# the app — and starts recording a durable, personal record of a pair that
# nothing previously kept. Both halves want a human read before they are live,
# the same call 202609070011 makes about money: the triggers because a mistake
# there is felt on every send, and the recording because an opt-in that begins
# collecting is not something to switch on for somebody else.
#
# The client is safe in the meantime and does not need this migration to work.
# together_timeline() and set_together_optin() keep their existing signatures, so
# an un-migrated production returns the derived half of the timeline exactly as
# it does today; the filter chips simply find fewer groups, and together_status()
# answers without event_count, which the client reads as "unknown" and phrases
# around rather than rendering as a confident zero.
#
# Applying it: run the migration in the SQL editor, then
# supabase/operations/schedule_together_purge.sql standalone (cron statements
# have aborted batched transactions on some projects), then delete these lines.
202609140034_timeline_events
